---
import Layout from "~/layouts/Layout.astro";

const issues = "https://github.com/AzmSquad/Squad-Kit/issues";
const connectedApps = "https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/apps";
const helperCode = "https://github.com/AzmSquad/Squad-Kit/tree/main/auth-helper";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · squad-kit"
  description="squad-kit runs on your computer. We keep no account of you and run no servers that hold your data. What squad-kit keeps on your computer, and how to remove it."
>
  <section class="container-page py-16 sm:py-20">
    <article class="prose-docs max-w-3xl">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p>
        squad-kit is a program that runs on your own computer. This page says what it keeps, where it keeps it, and how
        to remove it. Last changed: November 2026.
      </p>

      <h2>squad-kit runs on your computer</h2>
      <p>
        squad-kit, its board and the AI agents it starts all run on your computer. <strong>We keep no account of you,
        and we run no servers that hold your data.</strong> Your projects, stories and code stay on your computer. They
        go only to the services you connect yourself: your tracker (such as Jira), your code host (such as GitHub) and
        the AI tool you choose.
      </p>

      <h2>The sign-in helper</h2>
      <p>
        When you press <strong>Sign in with Atlassian</strong>, Atlassian sends you back through a small helper at
        <code>auth.squad-kit.com</code>. Atlassian asks every app for its own secret during sign-in, and an app you
        download cannot hold a secret. So the helper adds squad-kit's secret and passes the sign-in straight on to
        squad-kit on your computer.
      </p>
      <p>
        squad-kit also uses the helper to <strong>renew the sign-in, about once an hour</strong> while you use it: the
        renewal passes through <code>auth.squad-kit.com</code> too. The helper runs on Cloudflare. It
        <strong>keeps no codes and no tokens, and writes no logs</strong>. To stop abuse, Cloudflare counts the requests
        from each internet address for about a minute; nothing else is kept. Its code is public:
        <a href={helperCode} target="_blank" rel="noopener">auth-helper in squad-kit's repository</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What squad-kit keeps on your computer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your Atlassian sign-in:</strong> a sign-in token, the Jira sites you allowed and your account name, in
          <code>~/.squad-kit/atlassian</code>. Only you can read it. The short-lived token squad-kit uses to call Jira
          is kept in memory only, never in a file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A Jira API token you paste instead:</strong> in squad-kit's private files on your computer. For a
          project set up with squad-kit 1.x, that is <code>~/.squad-kit/projects</code>; for an older project, the
          project's <code>.squad/secrets.yaml</code>, which squad-kit keeps out of git. squad-kit can also read it from
          the <code>JIRA_API_TOKEN</code> variable instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your project's settings</strong> (your Jira site and project, never a token): in the project's
          <code>.squad</code> folder, which your team shares through git.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>squad-kit makes every Jira call itself; it never gives your sign-in or your tokens to the AI agents it runs.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>How far the agents are kept away depends on your computer.</strong> On a Mac, and on Linux with
        bubblewrap working, squad-kit's guard walls the agents off from your sign-ins and tokens. On Windows, on WSL 1,
        and on Linux without bubblewrap, squad-kit cannot wall them off yet: there an agent could read what your own
        account can read, so use squad-kit only on a computer you trust.
      </p>

      <h2>What squad-kit does in Jira</h2>
      <p>
        It reads your project's stories, boards and sprints, and it updates them as the work moves: statuses, comments,
        labels, new stories and bugs. It does this as you, with the access you gave it, and only for the work you start
        in squad-kit. Nothing of it is sent to us.
      </p>

      <h2>How to remove it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Disconnect</strong> on squad-kit's Connections page, or <code>squad disconnect jira</code> in a
          terminal, deletes squad-kit's Atlassian sign-in from your computer. It works even without a network.
        </li>
        <li>
          To take squad-kit's access away on Atlassian's side too, open your Atlassian account settings,
          <a href={connectedApps} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Connected apps</a>, and remove squad-kit.
        </li>
        <li>To remove everything squad-kit kept, uninstall squad-kit and delete the <code>~/.squad-kit</code> folder.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This website has no analytics. Its fonts come from Google Fonts, so your browser asks Google for them. The film
        on the home page is YouTube's player, which loads only when you press play.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about privacy: open an issue on <a href={issues} target="_blank" rel="noopener">squad-kit's issues page</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Layout>
